{"type":"target-event","projectId":"000458-model-compare-ch33-ep2","event":{"type":"admitted","attemptId":"cmtrlxw6i00312cz96eswrkfl","instructions":{"agentVersionId":"0dbac07a-9c33-4f02-906f-01065e57c36f","blockVersionId":"b0afa872-cfb6-4ece-a01e-ee66f3557e6a","digest":"9afe8339baaac0b7b458d17a73f43da8f6edb59c47154e4a132cbc1b7e0a521b","agentDigest":"bda39c132d881151c66319dda9bbb8ad3987fa805ec07ba45dba82731169222d","blockDigest":"fd99eae985d0eede5f64bf6b64f7332de21f0d97bd29f16e7e3b9a6cb616d8df"}}}
{"type":"target-event","projectId":"000458-model-compare-ch33-ep2","event":{"type":"proposal","proposal":{"summary":"Revise ch33-seq0-ep2 into a source-grounded English adaptation of the supplied Zacchaeus/Jericho excerpt: expand the reflection to retain the developed argument and concrete anchors, keep the bridge as a causal application, finish with a short distinct settle, and rebalance timings for human review only. Canonical video/subtitle evidence remains unavailable, so source-dependent checks are marked not_checked where required.","command":{"projectId":"000458-model-compare-ch33-ep2","expectedRevision":1,"idempotencyKey":"c4e7fef0-a631-4b9f-b07c-dfa487eee4f8","operations":[{"kind":"set-timing","itemId":"reflection","durationInFrames":1380,"timingLocked":false},{"kind":"set-speech","itemId":"reflection","speech":{"text":"Jesus does not pass through Jericho as though its old curse makes it beneath his notice. On his way toward Bethany, where he will raise Lazarus, he still contrives to do good by the way. So he stops at the very place where Zacchaeus has climbed a sycomore tree, hidden above the crowd because he is little of stature and eager to see who Jesus is. When Jesus calls him down and chooses his house, the scandal is not only that a rich chief publican is noticed. It is that grace sees a man the crowd had already summed up, and receives him before the murmuring around him is finished.","role":"reflection","suppressed":false,"voice":{"assetId":"fbad58bb-cefa-4c27-a01e-0ef0fa93ba92","versionId":"cmtretxe100042ctenx3jrmih","digest":"f88fcf65aa78e5c9cd5e9babe48ed2f6838b80fface62d77b3930cf8d11a5b2a"},"provider":"elevenlabs","model":"eleven_multilingual_v2","settings":{"speed":1,"style":0,"stability":0.5,"similarity_boost":0.75,"use_speaker_boost":true},"pronunciation":null}},{"kind":"set-text","itemId":"reflection","text":"Jesus does not pass through Jericho as though its old curse makes it beneath his notice.\n\nOn his way toward Bethany, he still stops beneath Zacchaeus's sycomore tree.\n\nGrace sees the man the crowd had already summed up."},{"kind":"set-properties","itemId":"reflection","properties":{"fontSize":50}},{"kind":"move-item","itemId":"bridge","trackId":"main","startFrame":1380},{"kind":"set-timing","itemId":"bridge","durationInFrames":1020,"timingLocked":false},{"kind":"set-speech","itemId":"bridge","speech":{"text":"That is why the complaint fails. Common fame is not righteous judgment: a publican is not condemned in the lump, a sinful past does not prove a sinful present, and the physician loses nothing by going where the sick may yet be healed. Christ goes to Zacchaeus not to share his wrong, but to seek and to save the lost.","role":"bridge","suppressed":false,"voice":{"assetId":"fbad58bb-cefa-4c27-a01e-0ef0fa93ba92","versionId":"cmtretxe100042ctenx3jrmih","digest":"f88fcf65aa78e5c9cd5e9babe48ed2f6838b80fface62d77b3930cf8d11a5b2a"},"provider":"elevenlabs","model":"eleven_multilingual_v2","settings":{"speed":1,"style":0,"stability":0.5,"similarity_boost":0.75,"use_speaker_boost":true},"pronunciation":null}},{"kind":"set-text","itemId":"bridge","text":"Common fame is not righteous judgment.\n\nA sinful past does not prove a sinful present.\n\nThe physician goes where the sick may yet be healed."},{"kind":"set-properties","itemId":"bridge","properties":{"fontSize":58}},{"kind":"move-item","itemId":"settle","trackId":"main","startFrame":2400},{"kind":"set-timing","itemId":"settle","durationInFrames":300,"timingLocked":false},{"kind":"set-speech","itemId":"settle","speech":{"text":"Take a quiet breath. Let that mercy settle.","role":"settle","suppressed":false,"voice":{"assetId":"fbad58bb-cefa-4c27-a01e-0ef0fa93ba92","versionId":"cmtretxe100042ctenx3jrmih","digest":"f88fcf65aa78e5c9cd5e9babe48ed2f6838b80fface62d77b3930cf8d11a5b2a"},"provider":"elevenlabs","model":"eleven_multilingual_v2","settings":{"speed":1,"style":0,"stability":0.5,"similarity_boost":0.75,"use_speaker_boost":true},"pronunciation":null}},{"kind":"set-text","itemId":"settle","text":"Take a quiet breath.\nLet that mercy settle."},{"kind":"set-properties","itemId":"settle","properties":{"fontSize":60}}]},"quality":{"coverage":[{"role":"reflection","status":"present","itemIds":["reflection"]},{"role":"bridge","status":"present","itemIds":["bridge"]},{"role":"settle","status":"present","itemIds":["settle"]},{"role":"hook","status":"absent","itemIds":[]},{"role":"conclusion","status":"absent","itemIds":[]},{"role":"question","status":"absent","itemIds":[]},{"role":"prayer","status":"absent","itemIds":[]},{"role":"quotation","status":"absent","itemIds":[]}],"findings":[{"criterion":"coherence","status":"pass","reason":"The revised sequence carries one argument from start to finish: Jesus deliberately stops in Jericho for Zacchaeus, the crowd's complaint is then answered by three source-grounded reasons, and the settle line closes on the same mercy without repeating the bridge verbatim. The opening starts with a claim rather than a recap-only retelling, and the bridge causally follows from the reflection's scandal of grace.","itemIds":["reflection","bridge","settle"],"sources":[{"assetId":"381ec520-6f36-45f4-9bb3-6e4bfb48b0c9","versionId":"cmtrkvcml00032ckj0hr3w28o","digest":"c2bb9fefb171e2af88ab2f2269bdf6a8645585ebf94e8481120f44b4a29d4127"}]},{"criterion":"depth","status":"pass","reason":"The adaptation keeps developed reasoning and concrete anchors from the supplied excerpt instead of reducing it to a generic moral. It preserves Jericho's old curse, the Bethany/Lazarus journey, the sycomore tree, Zacchaeus's small stature, the murmuring crowd, condemnation 'in the lump,' the distinction between sinful past and present, and the physician-to-the-sick rationale.","itemIds":["reflection","bridge"],"sources":[{"assetId":"381ec520-6f36-45f4-9bb3-6e4bfb48b0c9","versionId":"cmtrkvcml00032ckj0hr3w28o","digest":"c2bb9fefb171e2af88ab2f2269bdf6a8645585ebf94e8481120f44b4a29d4127"}]},{"criterion":"fidelity","status":"not_checked","reason":"The proposed claims were drafted to stay within the recovered pack excerpt, but the pack explicitly says canonical source footage/subtitles are not supplied and historic canonical-source provenance is unverified. Because exact canonical video/subtitle evidence is missing, full fidelity to canonical source cannot be confirmed.","itemIds":["reflection","bridge"],"sources":[{"assetId":"381ec520-6f36-45f4-9bb3-6e4bfb48b0c9","versionId":"cmtrkvcml00032ckj0hr3w28o","digest":"c2bb9fefb171e2af88ab2f2269bdf6a8645585ebf94e8481120f44b4a29d4127"}]},{"criterion":"voice_command_vs_appeal","status":"pass","reason":"The script avoids audience pressure and direct imperatives about conduct. 'Take a quiet breath. Let that mercy settle.' functions as a gentle settle invitation rather than a controlling command, and the rest of the sequence explains the passage instead of ordering the listener.","itemIds":["settle"],"sources":[]},{"criterion":"voice_third_party_address","status":"pass","reason":"The ending does not drift into judging a stranger's prospects. It keeps the focus on Christ's mercy in this scene and ends with a short inward-facing settle rather than commentary about what 'those people' should do.","itemIds":["bridge","settle"],"sources":[]},{"criterion":"voice_self_echo","status":"pass","reason":"No phrase or proposition is repeated within the reflection without development. The reflection advances from Jericho, to Bethany, to the sycomore tree, to the crowd's summed-up judgment; the bridge then adds new reasoning rather than restating the same claim.","itemIds":["reflection","bridge"],"sources":[]},{"criterion":"voice_opening_on_recap","status":"pass","reason":"The opening line presents an interpretive claim—'Jesus does not pass through Jericho as though its old curse makes it beneath his notice'—instead of delaying the main point with scene retelling alone.","itemIds":["reflection"],"sources":[]},{"criterion":"voice_scripture_echo","status":"not_checked","reason":"This check cannot be completed from the admitted evidence. The pack supplies a recovered excerpt but no canonical verse-card or canonical source-dialogue subtitle track for direct redundancy comparison, so scripture echo should remain not_checked rather than assumed pass or fail.","itemIds":["reflection","bridge"],"sources":[{"assetId":"381ec520-6f36-45f4-9bb3-6e4bfb48b0c9","versionId":"cmtrkvcml00032ckj0hr3w28o","digest":"c2bb9fefb171e2af88ab2f2269bdf6a8645585ebf94e8481120f44b4a29d4127"}]}]}}}}
